1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,520
Before we start, Spiros and Hiroko

2
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:07,009
thanks for coming in today to talk about your recent study experiences

3
00:00:07,009 --> 00:00:12,460
and congratulations to you both in doing so well in your first semester exams!

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:21,620
I'd like to discuss with you the value of the English for Academic Purposes course you did here last year before starting your university course.

5
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:24,303
Spiros, if I could start with you,

6
00:00:24,303 --> 00:00:29,670
what parts of the progamme have now proved to be particularly valuable to you?

7
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:34,450
I think that having to do a seminar presentation really helped me.

8
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:38,511
For example, a couple of weeks ago in our marketing subject,

9
00:00:38,511 --> 00:00:43,310
when it was my turn to give a presentation I felt quite confident.

10
00:00:43,310 --> 00:00:47,860
Of course, I was still nervous but because I had done one before

11
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:49,500
I knew what to expect.

12
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:54,100
Also, I know I was well-prepared and I had practised my timing.

13
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:59,264
In fact, I think that in relation to some of the other people in my group,

14
00:00:59,264 --> 00:01:03,460
I did quite a good job because my overall style was quite professional.

15
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:05,350
What about you, Hiroko?

16
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:07,834
Mmm, that's interesting. In my group,

17
00:01:07,834 --> 00:01:12,800
I was really surprised by the way the students did their presentations

18
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:16,940
they just read their notes aloud! Can you believe that?

19
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:22,130
They didn't worry about their presentation style or keeping eye contact with their audience -

20
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:27,690
and I remember that these things were really stressed to us in the course here.

21
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:31,950
So, how did you approach your presentation, Hiroko?

22
00:01:31,950 --> 00:01:36,330
Well, to speak frankly, I read my notes too!

23
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:40,458
At the time, it was a relief to do it this way,

24
00:01:40,458 --> 00:01:45,274
but actually when I had finished, I didn't feel any real sense of satisfaction.

25
00:01:45,274 --> 00:01:48,370
I didn't feel positive about the experience at all.

26
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:50,540
That's a pity. You know

27
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:54,350
although I was pleased with my presentation, I am not so pleased

28
00:01:54,350 --> 00:02:02,440
with my actual performance right now in the tutorials-during the whole semester I've not said anything in our tutorial discussions. Not a word.

29
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:07,420
Really, Spiros? Why's that? Do the other students talk too much?

30
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:12,570
It's partly that, but it's mostly because I have had no confidence to speak out.

31
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:15,360
Their style of speaking is so different

32
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:18,310
It's not the style we were used to during the course.

33
00:02:18,310 --> 00:02:20,250
They use so many colloquialisms,

34
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:26,070
they're not very polite and sometimes there seems to be no order in their discussion.

35
00:02:26,070 --> 00:02:28,679
Also, they are very familiar with each other,

36
00:02:28,679 --> 00:02:31,440
so because they know each other's habits

37
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:33,570
they can let each other into the discussion.

38
00:02:33,570 --> 00:02:37,370
You're right, Spiros, I've experienced that too.

39
00:02:37,370 --> 00:02:41,850
For most of this semester, I've said absolutely nothing in tutorials.

40
00:02:41,850 --> 00:02:46,551
But recently, I've been trying to speak up more and I jump in,

41
00:02:46,551 --> 00:02:49,940
and I've noticed an interesting thing

42
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:53,061
I've noticed that if they thought my point was interesting or new,

43
00:02:53,061 --> 00:02:56,677
then the next time they actually asked for my opinion,

44
00:02:56,677 --> 00:03:01,740
and then it was much easier for me to be part of the discussion.

45
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:05,312
That's great, Hiroko! I hope that happens for me next Semester -

46
00:03:05,312 --> 00:03:08,560
I'll have to work hard to find some interesting points.

47
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:11,110
What helped you to find these ideas?

48
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:15,250
I think that one thing that helped me with this was the reading.

49
00:03:15,250 --> 00:03:20,640
I've had to do so much reading this semester just to help me make sense of the lectures.

50
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:24,810
At first I couldn't understand what the lecturers were talking about,

51
00:03:24,810 --> 00:03:28,600
so I had to turn to the books and journals.

52
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:31,380
Every night I read for hours

53
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:36,572
using the lists of references that were given, and I made pages of notes.

54
00:03:36,572 --> 00:03:40,160
At breakfast, I read and read my notes again.

55
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:44,540
This habit has helped me to follow the ideas in the lectures,

56
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:48,920
and it's also given me some ideas to use in the tutorials.

57
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:51,220
But I did so much reading anyway

58
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:54,700
I don't think there's any time left over for anything extra.

59
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:57,720
My reading speed is still quite slow

60
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:01,030
though I'm much better at dealing with vocabulary than I used to be.

61
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:07,000
What else do you think we could add to the course program to help with this reading problem?

62
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,220
There's not really anything because it's my problem.

63
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:14,300
I remember we were given long articles to read.

64
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:21,630
We didn't like that but now I realise that reading those long articles was good preparation for the things I need to read now.

65
00:04:21,630 --> 00:04:25,361
Also, in class we regularly had speed-reading tasks to do,

66
00:04:25,361 --> 00:04:32,450
and we kept a record of our reading speed, so the teachers were encouraging us to work on that.

67
00:04:32,450 --> 00:04:36,920
That's true Spiros, but what we read could have been different.

68
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:40,297
Sometimes in the English class I felt frustrated

69
00:04:40,337 --> 00:04:45,241
when I had to read articles about the environment or health or education

70
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:48,780
because I wanted to concentrate on my own field

71
00:04:49,020 --> 00:04:51,580
but we didn't read anything about engineering.

72
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:56,510
So, I think I wasted some time learning vocabulary I didn't need.

73
00:04:56,510 --> 00:05:01,550
But surely the strategies you were taught for dealing with that vocabulary were helpful.

74
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:08,300
Yes, but psychologically speaking, I would have felt much better working on reading from my own field.

75
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:10,430
What do you think Spiros?

76
00:05:10,430 --> 00:05:13,160
I agree; that would have helped my confidence too

77
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:15,200
and I would have been more motivated.

78
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:18,700
It was good though that we could work on our own topics

79
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:20,340
when we wrote the research assignments.

80
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:24,000
Okay, let's move on to writing now...
